Understand What We are dong:
----------------------------
    PC0 ---- SW1 ---- R1 ===== R2 ---- SW2 ---- PC1

    Router R1 has two jobs:
    -----------------------
    1) Talk to the local LAN:
        PC0 - Switch1 - R1 G0/0
        So G0/0 must be in the same network as PC0
        Therefore: G0/0 = 192.168.10.1/24

    2) Talk to Router R2:
        R1 G0/1 ------- G0/1 R2
        So G0/1 needs another network: 10.10.10.1/30

Q) Which interface should receive this IP address: (G0/0 G0/1)
--------------------------------------------------------------
                     10.10.10.0/30
                G0/1                    G0/1
        PC0 ---- SW1 ---- R1 ---------------- R2 ---- SW2 ---- PC1
                 G0/0                    G0/0

        192.168.10.0/24              192.168.20.0/24

    Forget the IP address for a moment,
    Just look at the physical connections,
    Router R1 has two interfaces: [G0/0, G0/1]
    Which interface is physically connected to Switch1: PC0 --- Switch1 --- 0/0
    Which interface is physically connected to Router R2:
    >> Always assign IP address based on what the interface is physically connected to.

    Router R1:
    -----------
    G0/0 is connected to the LAN: PC0 -- SW1 -- R1 G0/0
    The LAN network is: 192.168.10.0/24
    Therefore: R1 G0/0 = 192.168.10.1/24
    Why: Because every device connected to the same LAN must belong to the same IP network
    G0/1 is connected to Router R2: R1 G0/1 ------- G0/1 R2
    Network: 10.10.10.0/30
    Therefore: R1 G0/1 = 10.10.10.1/30

    Router R2:
    ----------
    R2 G0/1 -------- G0/1 R1
    So: R2 G0/1 = 10.10.10.2/30
    R2 G0/0 - SW2 - PC1
    LAN Network: 192.168.20.0/24
    Therefore: R2 G0/0 = 192.168.20.1/24


























